\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 FREQ-NESS TOOLBOX \'96 README
\f1\b0\fs24 \
The FREQ-NESS toolbox allows you to extract frequency-resolved brain networks from EEG/MEG/iEEG data using Generalized Eigendecomposition (GED).\uc0\u8232 You can either:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Use each FREQ-NESS function individually, found in the \'91FREQNESS_Functions\'92 folder, \uc0\u8232 or\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Run everything automatically through the \'91FREQNESS_MainPipeline.m\'92.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Most users will want option (2).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 USING THE MAIN PIPELINE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 To run a full analysis on single or multiple participants, simply:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Place your data files in the appropriate folders inside \'93FREQNESS_Data\'94.\uc0\u8232 \'95 Each Group or Condition is a \'91Dataset\'92 folder.\u8232 \'95 Each participant is one .mat file containing a voxels\'d7time matrix.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 (Optional but recommended) Place a .mat file containing the voxel MNI coordinates (Nvoxels\'d73)\uc0\u8232 inside the \'91FREQNESS_MNI_Coordinates\'92 folder. These are required for spatial visualizations and gradient modelling.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Open the file:\uc0\u8232 FREQNESS_MainPipeline.m\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Scroll to the section \'93DEFINE ANALYSIS SETTINGS\'94 and edit:\uc0\u8232 \'95 frex (frequencies for GED)\u8232 \'95 srate (sampling rate)\u8232 \'95 network-estimation settings (duration, filter, regularisation, components, bad segments, and optional rescaling)\u8232 \'95 visualization settings (landscape, patterns, output paths)\u8232 \'95 entropy, exponential decay, gradient modelling and CFC options\u8232 \'95 backprojection and network-removal frequency/component settings\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Press RUN.\uc0\u8232 The pipeline will automatically:\u8232 \'96 Load all datasets\u8232 \'96 Run frequency-resolved GED via FREQNESS_NetworkEstimation\u8232 \'96 Produce network landscapes and activation maps via FREQNESS_Visualizer\u8232 \'96 Compute entropy and effective dimensionality\u8232 \'96 Fit exponential decay of eigenvalues\u8232 \'96 Model spatial gradients across frequencies and components\u8232 \'96 Compute phase\'96amplitude cross-frequency coupling\u8232 \'96 Backproject selected networks and remove them from broadband data\u8232 \'96 Re-estimate and visualize the cleaned network landscape when requested\u8232 \'96 Generate figures and save NIfTI images when requested\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 No manual intervention is needed after the initial settings.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 ALTERNATIVE: USING FUNCTIONS INDIVIDUALLY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you prefer a modular workflow, you can call each function separately:\
FREQ = FREQNESS_NetworkEstimation(...)\uc0\u8232 FREQNESS_Visualizer(FREQ,...)\u8232 FREQNESS_EntropyLandscape(FREQ)\u8232 FREQNESS_ExponentialDK(FREQ,...)\u8232 FREQNESS_FreqGradients(FREQ, MNI,...)\u8232 FREQNESS_CompGradients(FREQ, MNI,...)\u8232 FREQNESS_CrossCoupling(FREQ, lfo_freq,...)\u8232 FREQNESS_BackProjection(FREQ, freq2project,...)\u8232 FREQNESS_NetworkRemoval(FREQ, data, freq2remove,...)\
However, the MAIN PIPELINE already handles the correct order, settings, and group-level bookkeeping automatically.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 WHERE TO FIND FULL DESCRIPTIONS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 All functions are described in detail inside the header of the Main Pipeline file:\uc0\u8232 FREQNESS_MainPipeline.m\u8232 This includes the folder structure, required inputs, recommended settings, and interpretation of outputs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

